16:12:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s an excellent observation, and it gets to a nuance I glossed over. You’re seeing your fans spin up or even experiencing a bit of lag during playback, so it *feels* like the GPU must be working hard—maybe even doing “rendering.” But what’s actually happening is still quite different from game rendering, and I’ll explain why. --- ### 1. Video decoding is *usually* lightweight, but not always Modern GPUs have a dedicated piece of hardware called a **video decode unit** (often part of the “NVDEC” on Nvidia, “UVD/VCN” on AMD, or “Quick Sync” on Intel). This is a fixed-function block that’s purpose-built to decode specific video codecs (H.264, HEVC, VP9, AV1, etc.) with extreme efficiency—like having a tiny dedicated processor just for unpacking video. When you play a video that matches what your decode unit supports, the job is indeed a walk in the park. But several things can go wrong: - **Unsupported codec or profile:** If your video uses a codec your hardware decoder doesn’t support (e.g., a very new version of AV1 on an older GPU), the decode falls back to **software decoding**, which uses your CPU or, in some cases, GPU shader cores (general-purpose processing). That’s suddenly much harder work—like asking the entire orchestra to play a single flute note—and it will ramp up power consumption. - **Very high resolution or bitrate:** Even hardware decoders have limits. Decoding an 8K 60fps HEVC file will tax the decode unit far more than 1080p, and the extra memory bandwidth and data shuffling can make the whole GPU draw more power, spinning up fans. - **High frame rate content (e.g., 60fps vs. 24fps):** More frames per second means the decoder is working harder per second, even if each frame is simple. So your fans might spin because the decode unit is running hot—not because the GPU is creating 3D scenes, but because it’s still slinging a lot of pixels and bits around. --- ### 2. Post-processing: “enhancements” that cost extra Even when the video itself is decoded perfectly by dedicated hardware, your PC often does additional processing *after* decoding that can eat GPU power. Common culprits: - **Motion smoothing / frame interpolation** (like “soap opera effect”): Some apps or TVs insert artificial frames to make 24fps content look smoother. This is a heavy computational task that uses the GPU’s shader cores. - **Upscaling and sharpening:** If the video is 1080p but you’re watching on a 4K monitor, the GPU has to scale it up. Basic scaling is cheap, but high-quality upscalers (like NVIDIA’s RTX Video Super Resolution, or an advanced scaler in a media player) use AI models and run on the tensor/compute cores—definitely enough to make the fans spin and even cause a small frame drop. - **HDR tone mapping:** If your monitor isn’t true HDR but the video is, the GPU might be converting the signal on the fly, which can be compute-intensive. - **Color correction, noise reduction, or deinterlacing:** Some players apply these filters automatically, and they use the general-purpose GPU. This is the most likely answer to your lag/fan experience: your computer is doing extra work *on top* of simple video playback. --- ### 3. Browser vs. dedicated player overhead If you’re watching in a web browser (which is very common), the browser’s rendering engine can add overhead. Video often gets composited within a complex web page, and the browser may use inefficient decoding paths. Browsers are notorious for occasionally falling back to software decoding even when hardware decoding is technically supported because of DRM (digital rights management) or sandboxing issues. That can peg your CPU and make the whole system feel sluggish, while the GPU’s decode unit sits idle. --- ### 4. The crucial difference from “real rendering” In all these cases, the GPU is never *creating* the scene from a 3D model. It’s either: - Unpacking pre-made frames (video decoding), - Or manipulating those frames with filters/effects (post-